1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,120
 - Hello, and welcome to chapter eight.

2
00:00:02,120 --> 00:00:04,700
 We're gonna talk about lists in this chapter.

3
00:00:04,700 --> 00:00:07,860
 Up to now, we've been talking about algorithms.

4
00:00:07,860 --> 00:00:10,480
 Algorithms are the concept in computer science

5
00:00:10,480 --> 00:00:13,900
 of using the programming language to express the steps

6
00:00:13,900 --> 00:00:15,180
 that you want the computer to go through

7
00:00:15,180 --> 00:00:16,960
 to solve the problem.

8
00:00:16,960 --> 00:00:19,920
 Read some data, convert it to a floating point number,

9
00:00:19,920 --> 00:00:21,360
 check to see if it's greater than 40,

10
00:00:21,360 --> 00:00:22,660
 do one thing, if it's greater than 40,

11
00:00:22,660 --> 00:00:25,340
 do another thing, if it's not, then print out the result.

12
00:00:25,340 --> 00:00:28,920
 Or open a file, read everything,

13
00:00:28,920 --> 00:00:30,960
 if the first line starts with something, do something,

14
00:00:30,960 --> 00:00:34,120
 if not, skip it, and then add all the things up.

15
00:00:34,120 --> 00:00:36,740
 Those are steps, those are a series of steps,

16
00:00:36,740 --> 00:00:38,680
 and hopefully by now, you're getting to the point

17
00:00:38,680 --> 00:00:40,760
 where you have a good understanding of steps.

18
00:00:40,760 --> 00:00:43,800
 But there's a whole other side of computer programming,

19
00:00:43,800 --> 00:00:45,000
 and we call it data structures.

20
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:47,280
 And data structures is not the steps,

21
00:00:47,280 --> 00:00:51,320
 but instead, clever ways that you lay out the data,

22
00:00:51,320 --> 00:00:53,080
 and clever ways that you make sure

23
00:00:53,080 --> 00:00:55,700
 that the data does what you want it to do.

24
00:00:55,700 --> 00:00:58,120
 And so that's what we're gonna start talking about now.

25
00:00:58,120 --> 00:01:01,100
 Lists are the first and most simplest data structure.

26
00:01:01,100 --> 00:01:03,120
 Strings are kinda like data structures,

27
00:01:03,120 --> 00:01:06,200
 but lists are probably our first real data structure

28
00:01:06,200 --> 00:01:07,760
 that we're gonna think about and design

29
00:01:07,760 --> 00:01:09,640
 and make use of effectively.

30
00:01:09,640 --> 00:01:12,640
 But before we talk about what is a collection,

31
00:01:12,640 --> 00:01:14,500
 we should talk about what is not a collection.

32
00:01:14,500 --> 00:01:16,160
 So we're familiar with what a variable is.

33
00:01:16,160 --> 00:01:18,280
 We know that a variable is a little piece of memory

34
00:01:18,280 --> 00:01:19,940
 that's got a label on it.

35
00:01:19,940 --> 00:01:21,880
 And then an assignment statement, you know,

36
00:01:21,880 --> 00:01:24,500
 sticks a two into X, and then X is,

37
00:01:24,500 --> 00:01:26,640
 and then two is in this little cupboard.

38
00:01:26,640 --> 00:01:28,340
 And then it goes to the next line,

39
00:01:28,340 --> 00:01:30,260
 and then four goes into X,

40
00:01:30,260 --> 00:01:32,560
 and so the two goes away and the four is there.

41
00:01:32,560 --> 00:01:35,840
 A key thing is you can't have more than one variable

42
00:01:35,840 --> 00:01:37,700
 at any given moment, right?

43
00:01:37,700 --> 00:01:40,100
 And more than one value in a variable.

44
00:01:40,100 --> 00:01:41,920
 So when we move to collections,

45
00:01:41,920 --> 00:01:44,080
 collections are more like suitcases.

46
00:01:44,080 --> 00:01:45,660
 We can put lots of things in them.

47
00:01:45,660 --> 00:01:47,680
 We have ways of organizing them,

48
00:01:47,680 --> 00:01:50,140
 and as we go through lists and dictionaries and tuples,

49
00:01:50,140 --> 00:01:52,600
 we'll see how there are different ways to organize them.

50
00:01:52,600 --> 00:01:53,480
 And as a matter of fact,

51
00:01:53,480 --> 00:01:56,200
 we've been talking about lists for a while.

52
00:01:56,200 --> 00:01:59,100
 Every time we use one of these square bracket syntaxes

53
00:01:59,100 --> 00:02:01,620
 in earlier programs, we've been working with lists.

54
00:02:01,620 --> 00:02:04,500
 And so this is technically a three item list

55
00:02:04,500 --> 00:02:06,580
 with three strings, got commas here.

56
00:02:06,580 --> 00:02:09,020
 Joseph has one string, Glenn and Sally are another string.

57
00:02:09,020 --> 00:02:11,660
 And here's another one that is another thing.

58
00:02:11,660 --> 00:02:14,780
 And the list is basically, it's a list constant,

59
00:02:14,780 --> 00:02:16,220
 and it's being assigned into a variable.

60
00:02:16,220 --> 00:02:18,980
 So this friends variable has three things in it,

61
00:02:18,980 --> 00:02:23,420
 so that's different than what we've been talking about before.

62
00:02:23,420 --> 00:02:26,900
 So these bracket structures with square brackets

63
00:02:26,900 --> 00:02:28,340
 are those lists.

64
00:02:28,340 --> 00:02:30,580
 And so the print is just a print with parentheses

65
00:02:30,580 --> 00:02:31,820
 to get the print to work.

66
00:02:31,820 --> 00:02:36,300
 But 12476 is a three item integer list.

67
00:02:36,300 --> 00:02:39,720
 Red, yellow, and blue is a three item string list.

68
00:02:39,720 --> 00:02:41,960
 But it doesn't all have to be integers or strings.

69
00:02:41,960 --> 00:02:44,660
 Python can handle different things

70
00:02:44,660 --> 00:02:45,860
 and different kinds of data

71
00:02:45,860 --> 00:02:47,140
 in different positions in the list.

72
00:02:47,140 --> 00:02:49,900
 So red, 2498.6.

73
00:02:49,900 --> 00:02:52,600
 A three item list with a string, an integer,

74
00:02:52,600 --> 00:02:54,240
 and a floating point number.

75
00:02:54,240 --> 00:02:57,080
 And while we're not gonna use this too much for now,

76
00:02:57,080 --> 00:02:59,800
 this outer list is a three item list,

77
00:02:59,800 --> 00:03:02,820
 and the second item is another list.

78
00:03:02,820 --> 00:03:05,300
 So this is kind of eluding toward what we'll do

79
00:03:05,300 --> 00:03:07,460
 when we start talking about data structures.

80
00:03:07,460 --> 00:03:09,000
 And that is, we have a structure

81
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:10,360
 and then we have another structure inside of it.

82
00:03:10,360 --> 00:03:12,460
 And sometimes this can get quite complex.

83
00:03:12,460 --> 00:03:14,320
 And we're doing this for a reason.

84
00:03:14,320 --> 00:03:16,180
 This here has no reason,

85
00:03:16,180 --> 00:03:17,780
 just to show you that it's possible,

86
00:03:17,780 --> 00:03:20,860
 that lists can be made up of lots of things,

87
00:03:20,860 --> 00:03:22,160
 including other lists.

88
00:03:22,160 --> 00:03:26,080
 And of course, there is also the notion of the empty list.

89
00:03:26,080 --> 00:03:28,880
 And like I said, I have had to be able to tell you

90
00:03:28,880 --> 00:03:30,580
 about lists all along.

91
00:03:30,580 --> 00:03:32,300
 We use them in for loops.

92
00:03:32,300 --> 00:03:33,580
 We can put lots of things here.

93
00:03:33,580 --> 00:03:35,000
 We can put file handle here.

94
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:36,380
 We can go through the file.

95
00:03:36,380 --> 00:03:37,280
 We can put a string there.

96
00:03:37,280 --> 00:03:38,940
 We can go through the characters in the string.

97
00:03:38,940 --> 00:03:39,780
 And in the list.

98
00:03:39,780 --> 00:03:41,180
 And the iteration variable then goes

99
00:03:41,180 --> 00:03:43,500
 through the successive elements of the list.

100
00:03:43,500 --> 00:03:46,180
 And that's why this prints out five, four, three, two, one.

101
00:03:46,180 --> 00:03:48,860
 And then the loop is done and it prints out blast off.

102
00:03:48,860 --> 00:03:49,960
 So we've been using them,

103
00:03:49,960 --> 00:03:52,320
 and we've been actually iterating through lists

104
00:03:52,320 --> 00:03:55,540
 with for statements all along.

105
00:03:55,540 --> 00:03:59,160
 So the for each, I mean the for statement,

106
00:03:59,160 --> 00:04:01,500
 you know, has been something we use with lists.

107
00:04:01,500 --> 00:04:03,860
 And when you just need to go iterate through the list

108
00:04:03,860 --> 00:04:06,060
 and go through every item in order,

109
00:04:06,060 --> 00:04:08,100
 the for is a great way to do that.

110
00:04:08,100 --> 00:04:10,220
 So friend is our iteration variable.

111
00:04:10,220 --> 00:04:12,300
 Friends is our list variable.

112
00:04:12,300 --> 00:04:14,840
 And so that says friend is gonna successfully take

113
00:04:14,840 --> 00:04:17,380
 on the value Joseph, Glen, and Sally,

114
00:04:17,380 --> 00:04:20,240
 and print out, you know, happy new year, Joseph,

115
00:04:20,240 --> 00:04:21,080
 Glen, and Sally.

116
00:04:21,080 --> 00:04:23,240
 It runs three times, once for each of the values,

117
00:04:23,240 --> 00:04:25,460
 and the iteration variable advances.

118
00:04:25,460 --> 00:04:28,860
 Now, I do wanna make it really clear

119
00:04:28,860 --> 00:04:33,700
 that the choice of friends-a and friend-a,

120
00:04:33,700 --> 00:04:36,300
 singular and plural, is arbitrary and capricious.

121
00:04:36,300 --> 00:04:39,600
 It happens to be convenient and intuitive

122
00:04:39,600 --> 00:04:42,100
 that the iteration variable is one,

123
00:04:42,100 --> 00:04:44,380
 and the list variable is more than one.

124
00:04:44,380 --> 00:04:46,940
 But Python has no idea about singular and plural.

125
00:04:46,940 --> 00:04:48,780
 As a matter of fact, Python would care.

126
00:04:48,780 --> 00:04:51,100
 It would be totally equivalent for Python

127
00:04:51,100 --> 00:04:53,740
 to do the same thing, to have the list variable be z,

128
00:04:53,740 --> 00:04:55,680
 and the iteration variable be x.

129
00:04:55,680 --> 00:04:58,900
 X will take on the successive values of these three things.

130
00:04:58,900 --> 00:05:02,020
 Now, am I being nice to you by calling this list friends,

131
00:05:02,020 --> 00:05:04,160
 and this iteration variable friend?

132
00:05:04,160 --> 00:05:06,300
 I am, but I also don't want it to confuse you

133
00:05:06,300 --> 00:05:08,100
 if you're just a beginning developer.

134
00:05:08,100 --> 00:05:12,620
 So, just like strings, we can sort of look within lists.

135
00:05:12,620 --> 00:05:15,020
 Part of the thing is when you put more than one thing

136
00:05:15,020 --> 00:05:17,980
 in a data structure, you need to get them out.

137
00:05:17,980 --> 00:05:22,020
 And so, lists have positions, they maintain order,

138
00:05:22,020 --> 00:05:23,540
 and so the first thing in the list

139
00:05:23,540 --> 00:05:26,340
 is the sub-zero position, sub-one, sub-two.

140
00:05:26,340 --> 00:05:28,260
 Just like strings, they're zero-based.

141
00:05:28,260 --> 00:05:31,100
 Just like European elevators, they're zero-based.

142
00:05:31,100 --> 00:05:34,180
 So, if we take a look and we say, oh, friends sub-one,

143
00:05:34,180 --> 00:05:36,580
 that's how I read that, the little square brackets.

144
00:05:36,580 --> 00:05:39,060
 When you take a variable here and you say friends sub-one,

145
00:05:39,060 --> 00:05:41,540
 remember, singular and plural don't matter.

146
00:05:41,540 --> 00:05:44,660
 Friends sub-one means Glenn, 'cause this is the zero

147
00:05:44,660 --> 00:05:47,180
 and that's the one, and then Sally's the sub-two,

148
00:05:47,180 --> 00:05:48,980
 and so that's what prints Glenn out

149
00:05:48,980 --> 00:05:51,860
 in this particular thing.

150
00:05:51,860 --> 00:05:53,660
 Now, lists are mutable.

151
00:05:53,660 --> 00:05:55,300
 Mutable is another word for changeable.

152
00:05:55,300 --> 00:05:58,260
 They can be changed, meaning that a list has three things.

153
00:05:58,260 --> 00:06:00,860
 You can change this thing right in the middle if you want.

154
00:06:00,860 --> 00:06:02,660
 To take a look at what's not mutable,

155
00:06:02,660 --> 00:06:03,860
 strings are not mutable.

156
00:06:03,860 --> 00:06:06,960
 So, if I take a look at assigning banana into fruit,

157
00:06:06,960 --> 00:06:09,460
 well, fruit sub-zero is a capital letter B.

158
00:06:09,460 --> 00:06:11,060
 Could we imagine, for the moment,

159
00:06:11,060 --> 00:06:15,220
 that we could change fruit sub-zero to lowercase b?

160
00:06:15,220 --> 00:06:17,120
 Well, the syntax would be how you would do it

161
00:06:17,120 --> 00:06:18,580
 if you could do it, but it turns out

162
00:06:18,580 --> 00:06:21,780
 that strings are not mutable,

163
00:06:21,780 --> 00:06:24,300
 meaning they're not changeable once you create them.

164
00:06:24,300 --> 00:06:27,640
 And that's why when we do things like lowercase or uppercase,

165
00:06:27,640 --> 00:06:30,020
 we take a look at the fruit and we say,

166
00:06:30,020 --> 00:06:31,500
 give me a lowercase copy of that,

167
00:06:31,500 --> 00:06:33,420
 and then we take the return value from this

168
00:06:33,420 --> 00:06:34,780
 and we store that in x,

169
00:06:34,780 --> 00:06:37,500
 and that's how x becomes a lowercase banana.

170
00:06:37,500 --> 00:06:40,140
 But fruit is still the original one.

171
00:06:40,140 --> 00:06:42,180
 So fruit has not changed.

172
00:06:42,180 --> 00:06:46,580
 Compare and contrast that with a list, though.

173
00:06:46,580 --> 00:06:50,380
 Here we have a five-item list, two, 14, 26, 41.

174
00:06:50,380 --> 00:06:53,420
 And we're gonna do the sub-two position,

175
00:06:53,420 --> 00:06:55,780
 and the sub-two is zero, one, two.

176
00:06:55,780 --> 00:06:57,060
 So that's that one right there.

177
00:06:57,060 --> 00:06:59,660
 And we're going to assign a 28 into it.

178
00:06:59,660 --> 00:07:01,460
 So that 28 is going in here.

179
00:07:01,460 --> 00:07:03,620
 Gonna wipe that out and put 28 in.

180
00:07:03,620 --> 00:07:06,540
 So we can do item assignment in lists

181
00:07:06,540 --> 00:07:10,060
 by putting a bracket syntax on the left-hand side

182
00:07:10,060 --> 00:07:11,860
 to say don't just put it in the variable,

183
00:07:11,860 --> 00:07:13,820
 put it in this position within the variable.

184
00:07:13,820 --> 00:07:15,380
 So that's what that's doing.

185
00:07:15,380 --> 00:07:16,860
 And when you print that out, the 28.

186
00:07:16,860 --> 00:07:18,400
 Everything else is unchanged.

187
00:07:18,400 --> 00:07:19,620
 I mean, the whole list is there.

188
00:07:19,620 --> 00:07:21,460
 There could be 1,000 items in the list.

189
00:07:21,460 --> 00:07:23,680
 And then you're changing the second one.

190
00:07:23,680 --> 00:07:27,540
 We have a function called len.

191
00:07:27,540 --> 00:07:30,280
 We've been using this len function all along

192
00:07:30,280 --> 00:07:32,140
 to take a look at how long strings are.

193
00:07:32,140 --> 00:07:33,680
 It counts the number of characters in the string,

194
00:07:33,680 --> 00:07:35,580
 so that's a nine-character string.

195
00:07:35,580 --> 00:07:37,680
 If we have items in a list,

196
00:07:37,680 --> 00:07:39,580
 len tells us how many items there are.

197
00:07:39,580 --> 00:07:41,300
 It's not like how many characters there are.

198
00:07:41,300 --> 00:07:43,220
 It's the number of things.

199
00:07:43,220 --> 00:07:45,300
 And each thing doesn't have to be a number.

200
00:07:45,300 --> 00:07:47,420
 It could be a number, a string, or even another list.

201
00:07:47,420 --> 00:07:48,660
 And len is the way to say,

202
00:07:48,660 --> 00:07:50,360
 hey, how many things are in there?

203
00:07:50,360 --> 00:07:56,420
 There's a function that returns a list of numbers.

204
00:07:56,420 --> 00:07:58,500
 And we use it, as we'll see in a second,

205
00:07:58,500 --> 00:08:00,740
 to construct specialized loops to go through lists.

206
00:08:00,740 --> 00:08:02,540
 So let's take a look at this range function

207
00:08:02,540 --> 00:08:03,820
 just for a minute.

208
00:08:03,820 --> 00:08:05,540
 So range takes as its parameter

209
00:08:05,540 --> 00:08:08,380
 the number of numbers that you want returned.

210
00:08:08,380 --> 00:08:11,260
 So I want like, I'd like a four-item list

211
00:08:11,260 --> 00:08:14,580
 with the numbers zero, up to, but not including four.

212
00:08:14,580 --> 00:08:17,460
 And so it just turns out that that is really useful

213
00:08:17,460 --> 00:08:21,500
 for constructing for loops that are counted for loops

214
00:08:21,500 --> 00:08:23,700
 that go to zero, to the one, to the two,

215
00:08:23,700 --> 00:08:26,140
 as compared to the definite loops

216
00:08:26,140 --> 00:08:29,500
 that go through each one.

217
00:08:29,500 --> 00:08:31,260
 And so it's a common thing to say,

218
00:08:31,260 --> 00:08:33,500
 okay, we know how many things are in this list.

219
00:08:33,500 --> 00:08:34,860
 There are three friends.

220
00:08:34,860 --> 00:08:37,860
 And if I put combined range and len,

221
00:08:37,860 --> 00:08:40,020
 so I take len friends, which is three,

222
00:08:40,020 --> 00:08:42,100
 and then I take range sub three,

223
00:08:42,100 --> 00:08:43,700
 I get zero, one, and two.

224
00:08:43,700 --> 00:08:44,860
 And so the interesting thing

225
00:08:44,860 --> 00:08:47,180
 is this zero corresponds to the first one,

226
00:08:47,180 --> 00:08:48,780
 one corresponds to the second one,

227
00:08:48,780 --> 00:08:52,100
 and two corresponds to the third one, okay?

228
00:08:52,100 --> 00:08:56,160
 And so we'll use this to construct loops,

229
00:08:56,160 --> 00:08:59,780
 especially when we need to go through a,

230
00:08:59,780 --> 00:09:01,740
 we need to go through an array

231
00:09:01,740 --> 00:09:03,820
 and remember what position we're at.

232
00:09:03,820 --> 00:09:06,620
 And so here's just an example of two different loops.

233
00:09:07,700 --> 00:09:10,380
 This is a for loop that's just gonna go through

234
00:09:10,380 --> 00:09:11,500
 whatever's in this list,

235
00:09:11,500 --> 00:09:14,460
 so friend is just gonna take on the successive values,

236
00:09:14,460 --> 00:09:16,660
 and so it's gonna print out these three things

237
00:09:16,660 --> 00:09:18,300
 just as you would expect.

238
00:09:18,300 --> 00:09:19,700
 And if you don't need to,

239
00:09:19,700 --> 00:09:20,620
 while you're going through the loop,

240
00:09:20,620 --> 00:09:22,420
 know the position, your relative position

241
00:09:22,420 --> 00:09:25,540
 from the top in the loop, that's okay.

242
00:09:25,540 --> 00:09:28,340
 But sometimes you want a little more sophisticated loop,

243
00:09:28,340 --> 00:09:31,540
 and instead you want to be able to

244
00:09:31,540 --> 00:09:35,340
 loop through where you know the position.

245
00:09:35,340 --> 00:09:37,860
 And so what we do instead is instead of looping through

246
00:09:37,860 --> 00:09:40,820
 that list itself, we do range lend friends,

247
00:09:40,820 --> 00:09:43,620
 which gives us zero, one, two.

248
00:09:43,620 --> 00:09:46,340
 And then i takes on the successive values,

249
00:09:46,340 --> 00:09:48,220
 zero, one, and then two.

250
00:09:48,220 --> 00:09:49,980
 So this loop is gonna run four times,

251
00:09:49,980 --> 00:09:51,540
 and i is zero the first time.

252
00:09:51,540 --> 00:09:54,000
 And we might even just look up the value

253
00:09:54,000 --> 00:09:56,580
 inside that sub-zero value,

254
00:09:56,580 --> 00:09:58,300
 so we get Joseph the first time,

255
00:09:58,300 --> 00:10:00,020
 so prints out happy new year Joseph,

256
00:10:00,020 --> 00:10:02,740
 goes and i becomes one now,

257
00:10:02,740 --> 00:10:03,980
 and so it gives us Glenn,

258
00:10:03,980 --> 00:10:05,660
 and that prints out and away you go.

259
00:10:05,660 --> 00:10:07,420
 So if you look at these two loops,

260
00:10:07,420 --> 00:10:09,860
 if you look at these two loops,

261
00:10:09,860 --> 00:10:11,900
 they really do the exact same thing.

262
00:10:11,900 --> 00:10:14,120
 The only difference is this we allowed the four

263
00:10:14,120 --> 00:10:16,500
 to find its way with iteration variable through,

264
00:10:16,500 --> 00:10:19,340
 and here we created our own i variable

265
00:10:19,340 --> 00:10:21,260
 that went through the positions,

266
00:10:21,260 --> 00:10:23,140
 and they're dense, there's no gaps in here,

267
00:10:23,140 --> 00:10:27,100
 so it's zero through two that it goes through.

268
00:10:27,100 --> 00:10:29,420
 So these two are equivalent.

269
00:10:29,420 --> 00:10:31,700
 There'll be times when you'll want to use one and the other.

270
00:10:31,700 --> 00:10:35,220
 I tend to prefer the first one because it's prettier

271
00:10:35,220 --> 00:10:38,220
 as long as it works for me.

272
00:10:38,220 --> 00:10:40,740
 So that gets us started with loops.

273
00:10:40,740 --> 00:10:42,180
 We'll be back in just a bit.

274
00:10:42,180 --> 00:10:44,760
 (gentle music)

